{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788281150,"nonce":"ERD8j5XGY7kYtoLe6n9zQ1Vhg68=","message":"PROVEN END TO END ON A LIVE BENCH — CLOSING. Session 16, 2026-09-01.\n\nThe whole lifecycle, on one map, with one placement id:\n\n    build {def:\"Wall\", stuff:\"WoodLog\", pos:[120,120]}\n      -\u003e Blueprint_Wall 18072, placement_id \"pl-1\", journal seq 33\n         {\"verb\":\"build\",\"step\":\"blueprint\",\"placement_id\":\"pl-1\", ...}\n\n    construction\n      -\u003e state \"awaiting-materials\"\n         materials [{def:\"WoodLog\", needed:5, present:0, enroute:0,\n                     still_wanted:5}]\n         missing   [{def:\"WoodLog\", count:5, in_stockpiles:0}]\n\n    (a colonist hauls)\n      -\u003e kind \"frame\", state \"ready\", present 5, still_wanted 0\n\n    (a colonist builds)\n      -\u003e journal seq 55, type \"construction\":\n         {\"kind\":\"completed\",\"def\":\"Wall\",\"at\":[120,120],\"worker\":\"Lizzie\",\n          \"thing_id\":18369,\"placement_id\":\"pl-1\"}\n\n**COMPLETION IS AN ABSENCE, and the id survives it.** After the wall was built,\n`construction` reported `blueprints: 0, frames: 0` — nothing outstanding, as it\nshould. And:\n\n    construction {placement_id:\"pl-1\"} -\u003e state \"built\",\n        completed_tick 37157, thing_id 18369, present true\n    construction {placement_id:\"pl-2\"} -\u003e state \"cancelled\",\n        completed_tick null, thing_id null, present false\n\n(`pl-2` was a second wall blueprint removed with `designate {type:\"cancel\"}`.)\n`built` and `cancelled` are distinguishable **by field, not by parsing a\nsentence**, after both the blueprint and the frame have ceased to exist. That\nis the property this issue exists for and it could not have been proven any\nother way than by building something.\n\n**The map problem the worker flagged as its own highest risk is resolved.**\n`__instance.Map` is null in a `Frame.CompleteConstruction` postfix — vanilla\nitself does `Map map = base.Map;` then `Destroy()`, caching it locally for\nexactly that reason — so the hook takes it from `worker?.Map`. Had that been\nwrong, \"completions silently record nothing and every finished build reads\n`cancelled`\". It fired, and the row names the worker.\n\n`digest.construction` present and agreeing with the rollup:\n`{blueprints:1, frames:0, awaiting_materials:1, ready:0, in_progress:0,\nblocked:0, work_left:0}`.\n\n**The red-error hazards did not fire**, and that is now a real result rather\nthan an untested claim: `accept/s13-mod-surface.py` reports **174 PASS / 0\nFAIL** with a CORRECT watermark. (The first run of the pass showed three\nred-error FAILs; they were the suite's own defect — it had never received\nsession 11's `{since_seq: 999999999}` fix, so it counted from the start of the\nsession and charged the run for a deliberate error `journal-selftest` emits by\ndesign. Fixed in `19d6446`.)\n\n**Not demonstrated, said plainly:** `state:\"blocked\"` and `blocking_is_pawn`\nwere never exercised — nothing was dropped on a blueprint during the pass, so\n`GenConstruct.FirstBlockingThing`'s pawn clause (the sixth hazard, and the\nsharpest) is guarded by reading rather than by observation. Cheap to add to a\nfuture pass: drop a haulable item on a blueprint and read the row.","files":null}]}